+# Block placement: performance review
+
+Review of six suggested optimizations to
+`src/ServerScriptService/BlockPlacement.server.luau` and
+`src/StarterPlayer/StarterPlayerScripts/BlockPlacer.luau`.
+
+## The workload
+
+Start with how much work this system does, because that decides every item below.
+
+- The server accepts at most one placement per player every 0.1 s (`MIN_INTERVAL`), so 10 a
+  second per player.
+- A server holds 8 players, so the most it can ever do is **80 placements a second**, and only
+  if all 8 players click as fast as the rate limit allows.
+- Each placement is one remote call, a few table lookups, one `Instance.new("Part")`, and nine
+  property writes.
+- On the client, the code runs once per click or tap: one `RaycastParams`, one
+  `ScreenPointToRay`, one raycast, one `FireServer`.
+
+Nobody has reported lag and nothing has been measured. That is the normal case for event-driven
+code at this rate. Each placement costs the server microseconds, so even 80 a second is a tiny
+fraction of one millisecond per second. You only optimize a system when a measurement shows it
+costs too much, or when its design will clearly get there at its intended scale. Neither applies
+here.
+
+Several of the suggestions are good advice for code that runs every frame over thousands of
+items. This code runs a few times a second over a handful of items.
+
+## Verdicts
+
+| # | Suggestion | Verdict |
+|---|---|---|
+| 1 | Pool the block Parts | **No.** It cannot help: blocks are never destroyed, so nothing comes back to a pool |
+| 2 | Pack arguments into a buffer | **No.** It saves a few bytes on a remote that fires a few times a second |
+| 3 | Switch to `UnreliableRemoteEvent` | **No, and it would be a bug.** Placements would get lost |
+| 4 | `--!native` on both scripts | **No.** This code mostly calls engine APIs, which native code does not speed up |
+| 5 | Lookup table instead of `table.find` | **Done, but for simplicity, not speed.** The lookup table already existed |
+| 6 | Create `RaycastParams` once | **No.** It saves almost nothing per click and adds a respawn bug to handle |
+
+### 1. Pool the block Parts: no
+
+A pool saves the cost of creating an object by reusing one that was released earlier. These
+blocks are placed and never removed: nothing in the code destroys a block. So nothing ever goes
+back to the pool, and every placement would still need a new Part. All the pool would add is the
+pool code.
+
+Even if blocks could be removed later, a pool would still be the wrong call at this rate.
+Creating one anchored Part takes microseconds, and the server creates 80 a second at most. A
+pool only pays when a profile shows a burst of creation (dozens to hundreds of instances in one
+frame). It also costs something. It holds memory. A reused part can keep state from its last
+use (attributes such as `PlacedBy`, color, transparency). And a server-side pool of parts
+replicates to every client, which slows down joins.
+
+What is worth watching instead is the total number of blocks. Every placed block is a permanent
+replicated instance, and nothing ever removes them. Over a long server session that number only
+goes up, which costs join time and memory on every client. If lag ever shows up after long
+sessions, check `Stats.InstanceCount` and the size of the `Blocks` folder first. Pooling won't
+fix that. A cap on blocks per player or per server, or clearing blocks when their owner leaves,
+would.
+
+### 2. Pack PlaceBlock's arguments into a buffer: no
+
+A remote call costs about 9 bytes plus its arguments. Here that is a short string (a block name
+of 4 to 6 characters) and a `Vector3` (12 bytes), so about 30 bytes per call. At the 10-a-second
+cap, one player sends about 0.3 KB/s. A buffer might get that down to 15 or so bytes (a 1-byte
+block index plus three packed cell coordinates). That saves roughly 0.15 KB/s per player, far
+below anything a player could notice. Buffers save bytes and memory, not CPU time, and they only
+help when bytes are the bottleneck. They are not the bottleneck here.
+
+The cost would be real: hand-written encode and decode on both sides, a byte format to keep in
+sync, and new validation work on the server. A malformed buffer from an exploiter has to be
+rejected as carefully as the current `typeof` checks reject bad arguments.
+
+### 3. Make PlaceBlock an UnreliableRemoteEvent: no, it would break placement
+
+Unreliable events are not faster in any way that matters here. The difference is that they are
+**allowed to be dropped and to arrive out of order**. They exist for continuous data where a
+newer value replaces a lost one, such as positions and aim. A block placement is a one-time
+action, so if the packet is lost, the player's click does nothing. Players would see clicks
+silently fail on bad connections.
+
+The latency benefit of unreliable events shows up only when the reliable stream is backed up
+behind a lost packet. At a few small events a second, that almost never matters for this remote.
+It also has to stay a `RemoteEvent` because the client asserts
+`remote:IsA("RemoteEvent")`.
+
+### 4. Put `--!native` at the top of both scripts: no
+
+Native code generation speeds up Luau doing number crunching itself: tight math loops, buffer
+work, heavy table processing. These scripts do almost none of that. Their work is engine calls
+(`Instance.new`, property writes, `Raycast`, `ScreenPointToRay`, `FireServer`), and native
+compilation does not make engine calls faster. On top of that, the code runs a few times a
+second, so even a real speedup would save nothing measurable.
+
+Native code also costs compile time and memory against a budget shared by the whole game, and
+Roblox documents client support only on some platforms. Keep `--!native` (or `@native` on a
+single function) for the functions a profiler names as hot.
+
+### 5. Replace the `table.find` check with a lookup table: done, for simplicity
+
+The speed argument does not hold: `table.find` over 5 block names is a handful of string
+comparisons, a few tens of nanoseconds. A set beats `table.find` by a lot over hundreds or
+thousands of items, but 5 items is too few for that to matter.
+
+The change was still worth making, because the lookup table already existed. `BlockTypes.ByName`
+maps every valid name to its block type, and the handler was already reading it on the very next
+line. The `VALID_NAMES` array was a second copy of the same list, built at startup, just for the
+check. The handler now looks up `BlockTypes.ByName[blockTypeName]` and returns early when the
+result is `nil`. Behavior is the same: a name is accepted exactly when it is in
+`BlockTypes.List`. There is one less piece of state and one less list that could fall out of
+sync.
+
+### 6. Create the `RaycastParams` once: no
+
+Creating a `RaycastParams` and setting two fields takes about a microsecond, and it happens once
+per click. Caching it saves nothing a player or a profiler could see.
+
+It would also add a bug to avoid. The filter lists the player's current character, and the
+character is replaced on every respawn. A params object created once would keep excluding the
+old, destroyed character and start hitting the new one. To be correct, the cached version would
+need to update the filter on `CharacterAdded` or at each click, which is about the same work as
+creating it fresh, plus more code. The current version is correct by construction.
+
+## Summary
+
+The system does a small amount of work, at a low capped rate, with no reported or measured
+problem. Five of the six suggestions would add code and risk for no measurable gain. One of them
+(#3) would make placements unreliable. The one change made (#5) is a simplification that happens
+to remove a linear search.
+
+If performance ever needs a look, measure first:
+
+- **Server lag:** a MicroProfiler capture and a ScriptProfiler profile on the server while players
+  place blocks at the cap.
+- **Slow joins or memory growth after long sessions:** `Stats.InstanceCount` and the block count
+  over time. Unbounded block growth (see #1) is the most likely real cost in this system.
+- **Network:** `Stats.DataReceiveKbps` on the server. Expect placement traffic to stay well under
+  1 KB/s.
